window.ECBA_QUESTION_BANK=window.ECBA_QUESTION_BANK||[];window.ECBA_QUESTION_BANK.push(...[{"id":"q1","domains":[7],"level":1,"tag":"Applying standards in context","q":"A multinational organization wants every business analyst to use the Standard as an identical procedure, regardless of local regulation, initiative size, or stakeholder needs. A team lead asks how to respond. What is the best interpretation?","opts":["Use the Standard as foundational guidance, tailoring its application to the context and need of each initiative","Apply the Standard exactly as written first, then tailor only if delivery fails","Treat the Standard as optional background and rely only on local procedures","Replace the Standard with one organization-wide method so that every context is handled consistently"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. The Standard provides foundational guidance and says it should be applied with consideration for the context and need of each initiative.","The Standard does not require universal application before tailoring; context should inform how guidance is used.","The Standard is intended as a practical foundation, not merely background reading; local procedures may complement it.","A consistent organizational method may be useful, but the Standard does not require one method for every initiative."],"ref":"Chapter 1, §1.1, p. 1","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q2","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Using detailed resources","q":"A team understands the Standard's overview of a task but needs detailed examples and guidance for a certification study plan. Which approach best reflects the Standard's intended role?","opts":["Use the Standard as the sole source because it contains full task procedures","Use the Standard as a starting point, then consult the BABOK Guide and other detailed resources for the needed depth","Skip the Standard and search for examples without checking how they relate to business analysis tasks","Use the task cards as certification exam questions and treat them as a complete syllabus"],"answer":[1],"why":["The Standard is a simplified foundation and overview; detailed guidance is available in the BABOK Guide and other resources.","Correct. The Standard is a starting point and gateway to more detailed sources; its preface also directs certification candidates to the BABOK Guide and relevant handbooks.","Examples are useful, but connecting them to shared concepts and task guidance supports a more coherent study approach.","Task cards support understanding and practice, but they are not presented as exam questions or a complete certification syllabus."],"ref":"Chapter 1, §§1.5–1.7, pp. 2–3","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q3","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"Problem understanding","q":"A service team reports that customers are abandoning applications. Leaders request a redesigned form, but the analyst notices that abandonment rises only after a policy change. What is the best first analytical move?","opts":["Compare complaint trends across channels to determine which form fields are most often abandoned","Prioritize the requested redesign against other work based on customer impact","Measure abandonment after a redesigned form is released","Investigate whether the policy change explains the abandonment pattern before settling on a form redesign"],"answer":[3],"why":["Complaint patterns can help describe the issue, but this would not test the suspected policy-related cause.","Prioritization comes after clarifying the need and does not explain why abandonment changed.","Measurement can evaluate a later solution, but it would not address the suspected cause before choosing a redesign.","Correct. Business analysis seeks to understand underlying causes and context before recommending a solution."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §2.4, p. 7","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q4","domains":[4],"level":2,"tag":"BACCM relationships","q":"A new regulation prompts a policy change, which alters customer needs and requires changes to a service. The team discusses each item separately and misses how they affect one another. How should the BACCM help?","opts":["Use it to examine the relationships among change, need, solution, value, stakeholder, and context","Use it to classify the regulation as a requirement and exclude it from other analysis","Use it to select a solution by identifying which core concept has the greatest importance","Use it to replace stakeholder analysis with a single view of the change"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. The BACCM is an organizing and thinking model; considering relationships among all six concepts helps avoid an incomplete view of the change.","The regulation may influence requirements, but the BACCM is not a requirement-classification method.","The model is not a ranking tool; its purpose includes understanding the interrelationships and impacts among concepts.","Stakeholders remain one of the six core concepts and still need to be identified and understood."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §§2.1–2.2, p. 5","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q5","domains":[8],"level":3,"tag":"Value trade-offs","q":"Two options improve service for most customers. One option also reduces access for a small customer group, while the other costs more and takes longer. What is the most appropriate business analysis response?","opts":["Recommend the option with the broadest benefit and leave the smaller group's concerns for operations","Compare potential value and trade-offs, seeking to maximize value while addressing and managing stakeholder concerns","Avoid recommending either option because value cannot be delivered to every stakeholder","Choose the lower-cost option because cost is the clearest measure of value"],"answer":[1],"why":["Broad benefits matter, but the Standard also emphasizes addressing and managing concerns of stakeholders who may not receive value.","Correct. Business analysis aims to maximize value for the majority while understanding trade-offs and managing concerns for others.","The Standard recognizes that value may not be delivered to every stakeholder; that is a reason to manage trade-offs, not to avoid analysis.","Cost is one factor, but value includes worth and usefulness in context and cannot be reduced to cost alone."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §2.4, p. 7","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q6","domains":[9],"level":2,"tag":"Context","q":"A solution performs well in urban branches but poorly in rural branches. Interviews reveal differences in connectivity, operating hours, and local service practices. Which analysis lens best explains why one design has different results?","opts":["The value lens, because one solution should have the same value for every stakeholder","The need lens, because a problem or opportunity is the only factor that affects performance","The context lens, because circumstances such as infrastructure, processes, and environment influence the change","The change lens, because any transformation automatically produces different results"],"answer":[2],"why":["Value can differ by stakeholder and context; assuming it must be identical would miss the conditions described.","Needs matter, but the scenario highlights environmental and operational conditions influencing outcomes.","Correct. Context includes dynamic circumstances such as infrastructure, processes, and other conditions that influence and are influenced by change.","Change is a transformation in response to a need; it does not by itself explain why outcomes vary by location."],"ref":"Chapter 2, §2.2, p. 6","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q7","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"See the whole","q":"A team designing a claims portal begins with broad customer needs, such as understanding claim status and knowing what action is required. It then uses customer feedback to refine the emerging interactions. Which principle most directly guides this work?","opts":["See the whole, by considering the wider context and stakeholder relationships","Analyze to determine what is valuable, by prioritizing work for greatest value","Get real using examples, by clarifying needs through concrete scenarios","Think as a customer, by starting with customer needs and developing a detailed understanding to evolve the solution"],"answer":[3],"why":["Seeing the whole considers context and relationships, but the scenario specifically describes starting from the customer experience and refining the solution from customer needs.","Value prioritization may guide which changes to make, but the question describes how to understand and evolve a customer-facing solution.","Concrete examples are useful, but the defining principle is orienting the solution around customer needs.","Correct. Think as a customer starts with a high-level view of customer needs and develops a detailed understanding that evolves the emerging solution."],"ref":"Chapter 3, §3.3, pp. 10–11","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q8","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Feasibility and constraints","q":"A preferred solution would satisfy a high-priority need, but it conflicts with an operating constraint and cannot be supported by current infrastructure. Which principle most directly guides the analyst to examine this gap?","opts":["Understand what is doable","Analyze to determine what is valuable","Stimulate collaboration and continuous improvement","Get real using examples"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. This principle calls for continually considering constraints and whether a solution can be delivered while meeting prioritized needs.","Value is important, but the immediate issue is whether the solution can be delivered within constraints.","Collaboration may help address the constraint, but it does not identify feasibility as the core principle.","Examples can support shared understanding, but the main issue is operational and technical feasibility."],"ref":"Chapter 3, §3.3, p. 11","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q9","domains":[2],"level":3,"tag":"Systems thinking","q":"A team wants to optimize a local process. The analyst maps how the process connects to upstream data, downstream decisions, and enterprise objectives before recommending a change. Which competency is most directly demonstrated?","opts":["Problem solving, because the analyst is identifying a cause","Systems thinking, because the analyst considers enterprise relationships and context","Facilitation, because the analyst is helping stakeholders discuss the process","Adaptability, because the analyst is changing the solution approach"],"answer":[1],"why":["Root-cause problem solving may be part of the work, but the defining action is looking across connected enterprise elements.","Correct. Systems thinking involves holistic understanding of the enterprise and context to maximize delivered value.","Facilitation supports group interaction, but it is not the main competency illustrated by the relationship mapping.","Adaptability concerns adjusting behavioural style; changing a solution approach alone does not establish that competency."],"ref":"Chapter 3, §3.4, p. 12","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q10","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Approach selection","q":"A program has a fixed, regulated data migration with known steps and an uncertain customer portal that needs frequent feedback. Leaders ask for one approach for the whole program. What is the best recommendation?","opts":["Use a predictive approach for both parts because migration is regulated","Use an adaptive approach for both parts because the portal is uncertain","Use a hybrid approach, applying predictive and adaptive work to the parts where each fits","Delay selecting an approach until the portal requirements are complete"],"answer":[2],"why":["A predictive approach fits the migration, but the portal's uncertainty and feedback needs point to adaptive work.","Adaptive fits the portal, but the known, regulated migration may benefit from predictive planning.","Correct. A hybrid approach combines approaches across parts of an initiative, matching each to its context.","The approach should be planned in light of current context and uncertainty; waiting for all requirements could undermine appropriate planning."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §§4.2.1–4.2.3, pp. 14–15","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q11","domains":[7],"level":2,"tag":"Requirements and designs","q":"A stakeholder asks for a way to compare monthly sales across regions. The analyst records this need, sketches a dashboard, and learns in a review that managers also need to filter by product. How should the team treat this discovery?","opts":["The filter is a design change only, because it appeared during a design review","The requirement should be closed because a design has already been created","The dashboard replaces the need statement once stakeholders have reviewed it","The design may reveal new information that refines the requirement, so both can be updated iteratively"],"answer":[3],"why":["The filter may lead to design changes, but the review also reveals a need that may refine the requirement.","A requirement does not close simply because a design exists; both artifacts may continue to evolve.","A design represents a potential solution and does not replace the representation of the underlying need.","Correct. Requirements and designs are interdependent and cyclical; design work can reveal insights that enhance requirements."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §4.4.1, p. 16","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q12","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"Transition requirements","q":"A payroll solution meets its future-state requirements, but launch also needs employee training, data conversion, and a continuity plan. How should these temporary capabilities be classified?","opts":["Business requirements, because they state the objectives motivating the change","Stakeholder requirements, because employees and payroll staff need them","Solution requirements, because the new system must support them","Transition requirements, because they enable movement from the current state to the future state"],"answer":[3],"why":["Business requirements state the goals, objectives, or outcomes motivating the change; these items enable the transition.","Stakeholder requirements describe needs that help achieve business requirements, but the temporary launch capabilities fit the transition category.","Solution requirements describe solution capabilities and qualities; training, conversion, and continuity are temporary conditions for transition.","Correct. Transition requirements are temporary capabilities or conditions needed to move from the current state to the future state."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §4.4.2.4, p. 17","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q13","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"Security integration","q":"During analysis of a new customer-data service, the team identifies a security risk that could affect trust and compliance. The analyst is not a security specialist. What should the analyst do?","opts":["Avoid documenting the concern until a security design has been approved","Facilitate the involvement of security expertise and relevant stakeholders so risks and measures align with objectives","Specify the technical controls independently to keep the analysis moving","Treat security as a technical implementation matter outside business analysis"],"answer":[1],"why":["Security concerns should inform analysis and decisions early, not wait until after design approval.","Correct. The analyst can help the right stakeholders collaborate, identify security risks, consider measures, and align security with organizational objectives.","The analyst need not be a subject matter expert and should not independently specify specialist controls.","The Standard includes security integration as an organizational consideration for business analysis."],"ref":"Chapter 4, §4.5.1, p. 18","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q14","domains":[7],"level":1,"tag":"Task iteration","q":"Stakeholder elicitation reveals a new constraint that changes the team's understanding of the future state. A manager says the team must not revisit earlier analysis tasks. What should the analyst explain?","opts":["Tasks can be revisited, repeated, or performed concurrently as understanding develops","Only tasks without approved outputs may be revisited","The team must complete every task again from the beginning","The constraint should be recorded but cannot affect completed analysis"],"answer":[2],"why":["This is the right principle, but the most accurate action is to respond to the constraint through relevant task iteration rather than automatically reopening every task.","The Standard does not limit repetition to tasks without approved outputs; tasks may be reviewed and repeated as needed.","Correct. The Standard says tasks are not necessarily linear and can be managed, reviewed, and repeated as needed; the team should revisit the analysis affected by new understanding.","New information can change analysis and value understanding; it should be assessed for its impact on prior work."],"ref":"Chapter 5, §5.1, p. 21","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q15","domains":[7],"level":2,"tag":"Elicitation preparation","q":"A workshop must produce information for a decision, but participants have different levels of familiarity with the change. Before the session, what is the best preparation?","opts":["Start the session early and let discussion determine its scope","Confirm the workshop's desired outcomes and scope, choose techniques, and prepare participants, materials, and logistics","Conduct the workshop first, then decide which elicitation results matter","Ask stakeholders to approve a complete requirements package before elicitation begins"],"answer":[1],"why":["Starting without an outcome or scope can make the session unfocused, especially when participants have different levels of context.","Correct. Preparation defines outcomes and scope, selects suitable techniques, and plans logistics, materials, and participant readiness.","The purpose of preparation is to plan what the activity should produce and how; deciding afterward is too late.","Elicitation gathers information that may shape requirements; approval of a complete package is not a prerequisite."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Prepare for Elicitation, p. 28","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q16","domains":[4],"level":2,"tag":"Change strategy","q":"An enterprise has documented its current state and agreed on a desired future state. It is considering several transition states and a phased rollout. Which task should develop and assess these alternatives?","opts":["Analyze Current State","Define Future State","Assess Risks","Define Change Strategy"],"answer":[3],"why":["The current state is already understood; this task describes the existing situation rather than selecting a transition approach.","The future state has already been agreed; this task establishes desired conditions and objectives.","Risk assessment informs a strategy, but it does not develop and compare the alternative approaches for moving to the future state.","Correct. Define Change Strategy performs gap analysis, assesses approaches to reach the future state, and recommends an approach, including transition states."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Define Change Strategy, p. 41","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q17","domains":[6,8],"level":3,"tag":"Solution recommendation","q":"Three design options meet the stated requirements. One offers greater short-term savings, another reduces long-term operational risk, and a third supports a strategic opportunity. What should the analyst base a recommendation on?","opts":["The option that fulfills the largest number of requirements","The design with the lowest initial cost","The sponsor's preferred option, provided it is technically feasible","A comparison of potential stakeholder value, benefits, costs, risks, and constraints across the options"],"answer":[3],"why":["Requirement coverage matters, but it does not alone establish the greatest overall potential value or trade-offs.","Initial cost omits long-term benefits, risks, and constraints.","Sponsor preference is relevant input, but the recommendation should follow evaluation of all defined options.","Correct. The task compares potential value across design options, considering benefits, associated costs, constraints, and identified risks."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Analyze Potential Value and Recommend Solution, p. 47","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q18","domains":[9],"level":3,"tag":"Enterprise limitations","q":"A new analytics platform meets its technical performance targets, yet few teams use it because their reporting processes and roles have not changed. Which task most directly investigates this barrier to value?","opts":["Assess Solution Limitations, to examine factors within the platform that restrict value","Assess Enterprise Limitations, to investigate organizational factors outside the solution that restrict value","Measure Solution Performance, to define or use measures of solution effectiveness","Recommend Actions to Increase Solution Value, to propose changes that address identified limitations"],"answer":[1],"why":["Solution limitations involve factors within the solution; the scenario points to surrounding roles and processes.","Correct. Assess Enterprise Limitations investigates factors outside the solution, such as culture, operations, stakeholder interests, or reporting structures, that prevent value realization.","Measurement can quantify performance, but the scenario identifies an organizational factor restricting value beyond the platform itself.","Recommendations may follow the assessment, but the question asks which task investigates the enterprise-level barrier."],"ref":"Chapter 5, Assess Enterprise Limitations, p. 51","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q19","domains":[],"level":3,"tag":"Standard overview","q":"A certification candidate plans to use the Standard to understand the profession, then use detailed references to study tasks and knowledge areas. Which statement best supports that plan?","opts":["The Standard provides a concise foundational view and introduces 30 tasks, while detailed study should use the BABOK Guide and certification resources","The Standard contains the full set of knowledge area guidance and makes further resources unnecessary","The Standard is limited to definitions, so it cannot help someone understand task coverage","The Standard prescribes one sequence for all 30 tasks, which should be memorized as the certification syllabus"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct. The Standard provides an accessible introduction, foundational terms and concepts, and examples of 30 tasks; it points readers to the BABOK Guide and appropriate certification resources for detail.","The Standard notes that comprehensive knowledge area detail is in the BABOK Guide, so it is not a substitute for more detailed study.","It covers mindset, competencies, approaches, organizational considerations, and the range of tasks, not only definitions.","Tasks need not follow a linear sequence, and the Standard is not presented as an exam syllabus."],"ref":"Chapter 6, Summary, pp. 53–54","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q20","domains":[3,9],"level":3,"tag":"Planning analysis work","q":"A claims unit asks for a dashboard after processing delays increase. Before the analyst selects techniques or tools, the team needs to decide how its business analysis work should be conducted given the initiative’s risks, stakeholders, and context. Which task best fits?","opts":["Define Change Strategy","Plan Business Analysis Approach","Plan Stakeholder Engagement","Plan Business Analysis Governance"],"answer":[1],"why":["Define Change Strategy selects an approach for moving from the current state to the future state.","Correct: Plan Business Analysis Approach determines an appropriate method for conducting business analysis work in the initiative context.","Plan Stakeholder Engagement analyzes stakeholders and determines suitable collaboration and communication approaches.","Plan Business Analysis Governance defines decision-making and approval authority."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–31","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-complex-practice-set-4.html"},{"id":"q21","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"","q":"What is the primary purpose of planning the business analysis approach?","opts":["To define how business analysis work will be performed in the initiative context","To define the solution design that will be implemented","To approve the business need before stakeholders are identified","To measure solution performance after implementation"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: the approach sets out how BA work will be conducted, tailored to the initiative context.","Solution design is developed through other analysis work; it is not the purpose of planning the BA approach.","Approval of the business need is not the central purpose of this task.","Post-implementation performance measurement is not the purpose of planning the approach."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–30; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q22","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"","q":"Which is a core concern when planning business analysis governance?","opts":["How decisions about requirements and designs will be made","How detailed the future-state process models will be","How the solution will be tested against acceptance criteria","How the organization will measure benefits after deployment"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: governance defines decision-making responsibilities and processes for requirements and designs.","Model detail is an analysis deliverable decision, not a core governance decision process.","Solution testing is later solution evaluation or implementation work.","Benefits measurement is not the central focus of planning BA governance."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance, printed pp. 37–41; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q23","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"","q":"What does analyzing the current state establish?","opts":["An understanding of the existing environment and the reasons change may be needed","A complete specification of the preferred solution","A schedule for transitioning all stakeholders to the future state","A final evaluation of whether the solution delivers value"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: current-state analysis examines the existing situation and helps clarify the business need for change.","A preferred solution belongs to future-state and solution definition work.","Transition planning belongs to defining the change strategy.","Value evaluation occurs after or during solution evaluation, not as the purpose of current-state analysis."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q24","domains":[4],"level":1,"tag":"","q":"What is the principal value of tracing a requirement?","opts":["It links the requirement to related information so relationships and impacts can be understood","It confirms that the requirement has been implemented and tested successfully","It replaces the need to maintain requirement attributes","It establishes the authority for approving requirement changes"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: traceability records relationships among requirements and related items, supporting impact and coverage analysis.","Traceability can support verification work but does not itself prove successful implementation or testing.","Attributes and traceability serve related but distinct purposes.","Change authority is established through governance, not traceability."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.1 Trace Requirements, printed pp. 78–82; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q25","domains":[4],"level":2,"tag":"","q":"Which considerations can influence the business analysis approach?","opts":["The initiative’s complexity and uncertainty","The organizational culture and existing processes","The chosen solution technology alone","Stakeholder needs and applicable governance"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":["Correct: complexity and uncertainty affect how BA work should be planned.","Correct: organizational context, including culture and processes, informs tailoring.","Technology may be relevant, but it alone does not determine the approach.","Correct: stakeholder context and governance expectations help shape the approach."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.1 Plan Business Analysis Approach, printed pp. 24–30; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q26","domains":[4],"level":2,"tag":"","q":"Which items are normally clarified by a governance approach?","opts":["Who has authority to approve requirements or designs","How prioritization decisions will be made","Which modeling notation every stakeholder must use","How changes are submitted, assessed, and decided"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":["Correct: governance assigns decision authority.","Correct: governance defines decision processes such as prioritization.","Modeling notation may be selected for analysis work, but is not inherently a governance rule.","Correct: governance establishes how change decisions are handled."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance, printed pp. 37–41; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q27","domains":[4],"level":2,"tag":"","q":"Which statements describe useful directions or purposes of requirements traceability?","opts":["Backward tracing can connect a requirement to its source or rationale","Forward tracing can connect a requirement to related solution components","Traceability can support impact analysis when a requirement changes","Traceability proves that each linked requirement is feasible"],"answer":[0,1,2],"why":["Correct: backward tracing helps identify origins and rationale.","Correct: forward tracing helps follow requirements into related designs or components.","Correct: relationships help identify affected items during impact analysis.","Traceability shows relationships; it does not prove feasibility."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.1 Trace Requirements, printed pp. 78–82; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q28","domains":[4],"level":2,"tag":"","q":"Which factors may be considered when assessing a proposed requirements change?","opts":["Impacts on related requirements and designs","Potential effects on value, risk, cost, or schedule","Whether the change follows the defined approval process","Whether the request can be accepted without examining dependencies"],"answer":[0,1,2],"why":["Correct: related items may be affected and should be considered.","Correct: change assessment considers consequences such as value, risk, cost, and timing.","Correct: the governance process guides decisions about the proposed change.","Dependencies must be examined; ignoring them can miss significant impacts."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.4 Assess Requirements Changes, printed pp. 94–97; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q29","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"","q":"Which statements distinguish an enterprise limitation from a solution requirement?","opts":["A limitation constrains options or implementation; a requirement states a need the solution should satisfy","A limitation may exist independently of a proposed solution","A limitation should always be converted into a functional requirement","Assessing limitations can reveal risks to realizing solution value"],"answer":[0,1,3],"why":["Correct: limitations constrain the change or solution space, while requirements express needs.","Correct: enterprise conditions can exist before a particular solution is selected.","Not every limitation becomes a functional requirement; some are constraints, risks, or assumptions.","Correct: limitations can threaten implementation or expected value."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 178–182; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q30","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"","q":"A proposed change is small in wording but touches several linked designs. Which assessment principle is most appropriate?","opts":["Assess the change by its relationships and impacts, not by wording size alone","Treat linked designs as unaffected unless their owners submit separate requests","Approve the change first, then use traceability to discover impacts","Assess only the requirement’s source because downstream links are implementation details"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: impact depends on relationships and consequences, not merely the apparent size of the edit.","Related designs may be impacted even if their owners have not submitted requests.","Impact analysis should inform the decision before approval.","Downstream relationships are relevant to determining the effects of a change."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.4 Assess Requirements Changes, printed pp. 94–97; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q31","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"","q":"How does current-state analysis support a defensible change rationale?","opts":["It connects observed conditions and capability gaps to the business need","It assumes the desired future state is valuable because stakeholders requested it","It separates the business need from the environment that shapes it","It provides evidence about problems, causes, and constraints relevant to change"],"answer":[0,3],"why":["Correct: linking existing conditions and gaps to the need helps justify change.","A request alone does not establish value; the need and context require analysis.","Current-state analysis considers the environment that shapes the business need.","Correct: evidence about problems, causes, and constraints supports rationale."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q32","domains":[4],"level":3,"tag":"","q":"Which governance design best supports controlled, timely decisions?","opts":["Define decision roles, authority, and escalation paths","Require every stakeholder to approve every decision","Specify decision criteria and how approvals or changes are recorded","Leave decision rights implicit so the process can adapt informally"],"answer":[0,2],"why":["Correct: roles and escalation paths clarify how decisions are made.","Universal approval can create unnecessary delays and does not define proportionate authority.","Correct: criteria and records support consistent, traceable decisions.","Implicit rights create ambiguity rather than intentional flexibility."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.3 Plan Business Analysis Governance, printed pp. 37–41; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":true,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q33","domains":[4],"level":4,"tag":"","q":"Which sequence best represents a disciplined performance-improvement effort?","opts":["Assess BA performance, identify improvement opportunities, and recommend actions","Select a preferred solution, then redefine the performance measures to support it","Collect stakeholder opinions, implement every suggestion, and measure only adoption","Set improvement actions first, then determine whether any performance gap exists"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: improvement follows assessment and identification of opportunities before recommending actions.","Measures should provide meaningful evidence, not be redefined to justify a selected solution.","Feedback matters, but not every suggestion should be implemented without analysis.","Actions should be grounded in an identified opportunity or performance gap."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §3.5 Identify Business Analysis Performance Improvements, printed pp. 47–50; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q34","domains":[4],"level":4,"tag":"","q":"Which decision approach best preserves change control while enabling value-based decisions?","opts":["Assess impact and value, then route the proposal through defined decision authority","Use trace links to auto-approve changes with no downstream dependencies","Reject changes after baseline approval to protect stability","Allow the requester to update approved requirements when the expected benefit is high"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: assessment informs a decision made through the established governance process.","No dependencies do not automatically establish value or authority to approve.","Baselines support control, but changes can still be considered and approved.","Expected benefit should be assessed, but approval rights and change controls still apply."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.4 Assess Requirements Changes, printed pp. 94–97; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q35","domains":[4],"level":4,"tag":"","q":"When an enterprise limitation appears to rule out a solution option, what assessment is most useful?","opts":["Clarify the limitation’s source, scope, and effect on feasible options and expected value","Treat the limitation as permanent without confirming its nature","Ignore the limitation until deployment because it is not a requirement","Convert it to a stakeholder requirement and proceed with the same option"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: understanding the limitation and its impact supports sound option and strategy decisions.","Some limitations may be changeable or misunderstood; assess before treating as fixed.","Limitations can affect feasibility and value well before deployment.","Relabeling a limitation does not remove its effect or establish feasibility."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 178–182; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q36","domains":[4],"level":4,"tag":"","q":"Which conclusion is justified by current-state analysis?","opts":["A documented understanding of relevant existing conditions that informs the business need and change","A guarantee that a proposed future state will produce the expected benefits","A complete transition plan with release sequencing","An approved set of requirements for the selected solution"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: current-state analysis informs understanding of the need and context for change.","Analysis supports decisions but cannot guarantee future benefits.","Transition planning is part of defining a change strategy.","Requirements approval is a separate governance and requirements process."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.1 Analyze Current State, printed pp. 103–109; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q37","domains":[4],"level":5,"tag":"","q":"Which strategy decision best addresses dependencies among multiple transitions to the future state?","opts":["Sequence the transitions based on dependencies, readiness, risks, and the value of interim outcomes","Schedule all transitions concurrently so no interim state requires management","Sequence transitions only by estimated duration, then assess dependencies during implementation","Choose the highest-value component first without considering readiness or prerequisites"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: a viable strategy considers dependencies, readiness, risk, and value when sequencing transitions.","Concurrent transitions may be infeasible and do not eliminate dependency or interim-state concerns.","Dependencies can materially affect feasibility and should inform strategy before implementation.","Value matters, but readiness and prerequisites can constrain the feasible sequence."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §6.4 Define Change Strategy, printed pp. 124–130; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q38","domains":[4],"level":5,"tag":"","q":"Which conclusion is most defensible when measures show improved activity efficiency but unchanged business outcomes?","opts":["Assess whether the measures capture the outcome sought and investigate the relationship between activity and results","Conclude that the change succeeded because activity efficiency improved","Revise the desired outcome to match the available efficiency measure","Discard all measures because one outcome has not changed"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: measures should be examined for alignment with intended outcomes and interpreted alongside relevant context.","Efficiency improvement alone does not show that the intended business outcome was achieved.","Changing the outcome to fit a measure reverses the logic of performance assessment.","A divergence is a reason to investigate; it does not make every measure useless."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.2 Analyze Performance Measures, printed pp. 170–172; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q39","domains":[4],"level":5,"tag":"","q":"Which statement best describes the relationship among traceability, change assessment, and governance?","opts":["Traceability reveals affected relationships; assessment evaluates consequences; governance determines decision authority and process","Governance identifies technical impacts; traceability approves the change; assessment updates baselines","Assessment defines decision rights; governance discovers dependencies; traceability determines business value","Once traceability is complete, assessment and governance add no further decision information"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: each supports a distinct part of controlled change decisions.","These functions are incorrectly assigned to the wrong activities.","Assessment does not set decision rights, and traceability does not determine value.","Relationships alone do not evaluate consequences or establish an authorized decision."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.4 Assess Requirements Changes, printed pp. 94–97; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q40","domains":[4],"level":5,"tag":"","q":"Which reasoning best distinguishes a limitation that should shape the change strategy from an assumption that should be validated?","opts":["A limitation is an assessed condition affecting feasible options; an assumption is a belief treated as true until verified","A limitation is a belief awaiting validation; an assumption is a confirmed constraint","A limitation and an assumption are both approved requirements that restrict the solution","A limitation affects only the future state, while an assumption affects only current-state analysis"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: limitations describe constraints or conditions affecting options, while assumptions are beliefs that may need validation.","This reverses the distinction; a limitation is not simply an unverified belief.","Neither term necessarily represents an approved requirement.","Both may be relevant across analysis and change planning; the distinction is not based on state alone."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §8.4 Assess Enterprise Limitations, printed pp. 178–182; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 4: Change","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-12.html"},{"id":"q41","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"A team will elicit needs for a cross-border returns change. Which preparation decision should come first?","opts":["Confirm elicitation scope, desired outcomes, stakeholders, and suitable techniques","Choose a workshop because several departments are involved","Prepare detailed requirement models before contacting stakeholders","Ask the sponsor to approve a preferred solution first"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Preparation identifies the information needed, stakeholders who can provide it, and techniques suited to the objectives and context.","A workshop may be suitable, but choosing it solely because several departments are involved skips the objectives and participant needs.","Modeling detailed requirements before eliciting information risks turning assumptions into apparent facts.","Approving a preferred solution first biases elicitation toward a solution instead of understanding the need."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q42","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"For a time-critical policy change, key operational staff work rotating shifts. What is the best preparation?","opts":["Schedule one long workshop during standard office hours","Identify participant availability and choose techniques that fit the elicitation objectives and constraints","Send a solution design for asynchronous approval","Interview only the policy owner because the deadline is fixed"],"answer":[1],"why":["A single standard-hours workshop may exclude shift-based operational perspectives and does not account for participant availability.","Correct: Preparation considers objectives, participants, constraints, and techniques that fit the elicitation context.","A solution design assumes the answer before the BA has elicited the need.","The policy owner alone may lack the operational knowledge needed to understand the change."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q43","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"A BA plans to elicit needs about a current approval process. Which preparation is most useful before sessions?","opts":["Draft the future-state requirements so stakeholders can correct them","Review relevant existing materials and plan prompts around the elicitation purpose and stakeholder context","Ask every stakeholder the same unstructured question","Prepare acceptance tests for the expected solution"],"answer":[1],"why":["Drafting future-state requirements in advance can bias stakeholders toward an assumed solution.","Correct: Reviewing relevant information and planning purposeful prompts helps focus elicitation on the need and context.","Using the same unstructured prompt can miss role-specific knowledge and does not ensure the session meets its objective.","Acceptance tests presume that the solution requirements and solution have already been defined."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q44","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"Several stakeholders disagree about what “same-day service” means. What is the strongest preparation?","opts":["Plan focused questions and a suitable technique to uncover definitions, measures, and differing perspectives","Ask the sponsor to select one definition before elicitation","Use a poll to force an immediate majority choice","Write separate requirements for each definition without investigating context"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Focused questions and a suitable technique can clarify meanings, measures, and differing stakeholder perspectives.","Having the sponsor choose a definition before elicitation suppresses relevant evidence about stakeholder needs and context.","A poll may reveal preferences but can force a choice without clarifying what “same-day” means or how it is measured.","Recording separate conflicting definitions without investigating them leaves the need unresolved."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q45","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"After an interview, a stakeholder says the BA’s notes imply refunds must be immediate. The stakeholder meant “within the published service target.” What should the BA do?","opts":["Confirm the elicitation result with the stakeholder and correct the misunderstanding before using it","Preserve the original note because it was captured during the interview","Ask the sponsor to decide what the stakeholder intended","Convert the note into a measurable requirement without follow-up"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Confirming the result with the stakeholder checks that the captured information reflects their intended meaning.","Keeping a note known to be inaccurate preserves the misunderstanding rather than resolving it.","The sponsor cannot reliably determine what another stakeholder intended to say.","Adding measurement to an unconfirmed interpretation makes it precise, not accurate."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q46","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"A workshop produces a process sketch and several decisions. What is the best next action?","opts":["Send the sketch only to the sponsor for approval","Review the captured information with relevant participants, resolve discrepancies, and record agreed corrections","Treat silence after distribution as agreement","Ask the modeler to complete the process design independently"],"answer":[1],"why":["The sponsor may not be able to validate every participant’s process knowledge or the accuracy of the sketch.","Correct: Reviewing captured results with relevant participants helps identify inaccuracies, resolve discrepancies, and record corrections.","Silence after circulation is not reliable evidence that participants understood or agreed with the results.","Completing design before confirming elicited information can embed misunderstandings in the solution."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q47","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"Two users give conflicting accounts of an exception rule. Which response best confirms elicitation results?","opts":["Choose the account from the more senior user","Record both accounts as equally valid requirements","Return the conflict to relevant stakeholders with context and clarify what rule or need is intended","Average the two accounts into one rule"],"answer":[2],"why":["Seniority alone does not establish which account reflects the applicable rule or underlying need.","Recording both conflicting accounts as equally valid does not resolve which rule is intended.","Correct: Bringing the discrepancy and its context back to relevant stakeholders lets them clarify the intended rule or need.","Averaging interpretations can create a rule that neither stakeholder actually intended."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q48","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"A stakeholder approves a summary but later disputes a decision recorded in notes. How should the BA respond?","opts":["Treat the approval as binding and leave the decision unchanged","Check the specific result with the stakeholder and affected participants, then update the record based on confirmation","Delete the decision because it is disputed","Ask the delivery team to implement both alternatives"],"answer":[1],"why":["A general approval does not prove that the disputed decision was understood or confirmed.","Correct: Reconfirming the specific disputed result with affected participants allows the BA to maintain an accurate record.","Deleting a disputed result removes potentially useful information without establishing what was decided.","Implementing both alternatives avoids clarification and could create contradictory outcomes."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q49","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"A stakeholder says, “The system should make onboarding easier.” Which modeling move best supports understanding the need?","opts":["Select a standard onboarding workflow and ask users to accept it","Clarify the desired outcome and model relevant user, process, or information aspects to make the need explicit","Write “onboarding must be easy” as a functional requirement","Translate “easy” directly into a screen design"],"answer":[1],"why":["Selecting a standard workflow chooses a solution before understanding what “easier” means for these stakeholders.","Correct: Clarifying the intended outcome and modeling relevant aspects makes the need more explicit and supports shared understanding.","“Onboarding must be easy” repeats the subjective phrase without making the desired outcome clear.","A screen design is one possible solution and does not by itself clarify or validate the underlying need."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.1 Specify and Model Requirements, printed pp. 136–140; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q50","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"A regulation requires retaining a record, while users need to find it quickly. Which model best clarifies requirements?","opts":["A data model of required record attributes and relationships, paired with a process/use case view of retrieval","A technical architecture diagram selected by developers","A prioritization list without details of records or retrieval","A screen mock-up alone showing the search page"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: A data model and a process or use-case view can represent both the required information and how users retrieve it.","An architecture diagram selected by developers describes a solution structure but may not explain the business information need or user behavior.","A prioritization list ranks work but does not specify the records or retrieval behavior.","A screen alone may omit data relationships, retention needs, and process rules."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.1 Specify and Model Requirements, printed pp. 136–140; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q51","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"A written requirement says “notify customers promptly,” while a process model shows notifications only after manual review. What should the BA do?","opts":["Replace the requirement with a notification service design","Keep the statement broad and ignore the process model","Clarify and model timing, trigger, and exception behavior so representations express a consistent need","Ask testers to infer timing from the model"],"answer":[2],"why":["A notification-service design jumps to a solution without clarifying the timing need or the apparent process conflict.","Leaving “promptly” undefined and ignoring the process model preserves ambiguity and inconsistent representations.","Correct: Clarifying timing, triggers, and exceptions resolves the inconsistency and makes the need understandable.","Testers should not have to infer essential timing behavior that the requirement leaves ambiguous."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.1 Specify and Model Requirements, printed pp. 136–140; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q52","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"Stakeholders need to prevent duplicate case creation. Which specification best supports modeling?","opts":["“The solution should be modern and intuitive”","Draw an organization chart of teams handling cases","“The system must use a single database”","Define what counts as a duplicate, relevant matching data, and expected system response"],"answer":[3],"why":["“Modern and intuitive” is subjective and does not specify duplicate criteria or expected behavior.","An organization chart does not represent the information or behavior needed to identify duplicate cases.","A single-database constraint prescribes an implementation choice and does not define the business need.","Correct: Defining matching criteria and expected response makes the requirement specific enough to model and analyze."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.1 Specify and Model Requirements, printed pp. 136–140; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q53","domains":[5],"level":4,"tag":"Need","q":"A requirement says only authorized staff may view customer records. What is the best validation question?","opts":["Can developers implement it using their preferred access-control library?","Has it been ranked above all other security work?","Does it express a stakeholder need and align with relevant business objectives and constraints?","Does the screen design use the approved color palette?"],"answer":[2],"why":["Implementation with a preferred library addresses technical feasibility, not whether the requirement is aligned with the need.","Priority is a separate decision and does not establish that a requirement is aligned or valid.","Correct: Validation considers whether the requirement supports stakeholder needs and relevant business objectives and constraints.","Color palette compliance does not validate the access-control need."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q54","domains":[5],"level":4,"tag":"Need","q":"A proposed requirement would reduce manual review but may allow incomplete applications through. What should validation establish?","opts":["Whether stakeholders want automation in general","Whether developers can meet the target date","Whether the requirement supports the need and whether potential consequences and trade-offs are acceptable","Whether it can be copied from another organization"],"answer":[2],"why":["General support for automation does not show that this particular requirement appropriately addresses the need.","A target date addresses delivery feasibility, not whether the requirement is desirable or produces acceptable consequences.","Correct: Validation considers alignment with the need and whether the requirement’s consequences and trade-offs are acceptable.","Copying another organization’s requirement ignores differences in its needs, risks, and context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q55","domains":[5],"level":4,"tag":"Need","q":"A requirement is clear and testable but conflicts with a stated business objective. What is the best conclusion?","opts":["It is valid because it can be tested","It is valid if the sponsor signed it","Rewrite it only to remove the conflict from the wording","Evaluate alignment with the business need and objective before accepting it as valid"],"answer":[3],"why":["Being testable does not establish that a requirement supports the business objective.","A sponsor’s signature alone does not resolve a substantive conflict with the objective.","Rewording cannot remove a real conflict if the requirement still fails to support the objective.","Correct: The requirement should be evaluated for alignment with the need and objective before it is accepted as valid."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q56","domains":[5],"level":4,"tag":"Need","q":"A model passes its review checklist, but users say it omits a critical exception affecting the intended outcome. What should the BA do?","opts":["Accept it because the checklist passed","Treat it as a design issue and exclude it from validation","Validate the model against stakeholder needs and intended outcomes, then address the missing exception","Move the exception to a lower priority without impact analysis"],"answer":[2],"why":["Passing a checklist does not establish that the model includes all needs that affect the intended outcome.","Reprioritizing without understanding the exception’s impact could leave a critical need unaddressed.","Correct: Validation against stakeholder needs and intended outcomes exposes the omitted exception so it can be addressed.","Calling it a design issue does not justify excluding an omitted stakeholder need from validation."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q57","domains":[5],"level":5,"tag":"Need","q":"A release has limited capacity. One requirement is urgent for a regulatory date; another has larger long-term benefit. What should inform prioritization?","opts":["Assess relative value, urgency, risks, dependencies, and stakeholder context before ranking","Select the higher-value requirement automatically","Select the urgent requirement automatically regardless of dependencies or risk","Rank only by smallest implementation effort"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Prioritization weighs multiple factors such as value, urgency, risk, dependencies, and stakeholder context.","Choosing by value alone may overlook a regulatory deadline, dependencies, and risk.","Choosing by urgency alone ignores relative value, dependencies, and risk.","Effort can inform a decision, but ranking only by smallest size may fail to maximize value."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.3 Prioritize Requirements, printed pp. 86–90; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q58","domains":[5],"level":5,"tag":"Need","q":"A stakeholder asks to prioritize a feature because it is highly visible. What is the best response?","opts":["Rank it first to protect stakeholder satisfaction","Delay ranking until every requirement is fully specified","Reject it because visibility is not a business factor","Evaluate value and urgency alongside risks, dependencies, and other stakeholders’ needs"],"answer":[3],"why":["Visibility alone does not establish that the feature has the greatest relative importance.","Prioritization can be iterative; it need not wait until every requirement is fully detailed.","Visibility may be relevant, so rejecting it without examining value and context is also arbitrary.","Correct: Evaluating value and urgency alongside risks, dependencies, and stakeholder needs supports a contextual ranking."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.3 Prioritize Requirements, printed pp. 86–90; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q59","domains":[5],"level":5,"tag":"Need","q":"A lower-priority requirement is a prerequisite for a high-value one. How should the analyst treat this dependency?","opts":["Keep it below because its standalone value is limited","Raise every prerequisite to exactly the same priority","Remove the high-value item from consideration","Consider the dependency when assessing sequence and relative priority of both items"],"answer":[3],"why":["Looking only at standalone value misses the enabling role of a prerequisite for the higher-value item.","Prerequisites may have different value, risk, and urgency, so they do not automatically receive identical priority.","A prerequisite does not automatically invalidate the dependent high-value requirement; assess the relationship.","Correct: Dependencies can affect both relative priority and implementation sequence."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.3 Prioritize Requirements, printed pp. 86–90; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q60","domains":[5],"level":5,"tag":"Need","q":"New evidence midway through delivery reduces the expected benefit of a previously top-ranked requirement. What should the BA recommend?","opts":["Keep the original ranking to preserve consistency","Continue because prioritization happens only before delivery","Move it to the bottom without consultation","Reassess priorities with stakeholders using updated value, urgency, risk, and dependency information"],"answer":[3],"why":["Preserving consistency is not a reason to ignore evidence that changes expected value.","Priorities can be revisited throughout the life cycle as context and evidence change.","Moving an item to the bottom unilaterally may ignore stakeholder impacts and dependencies.","Correct: Prioritization is ongoing; stakeholders should reassess using current value, urgency, risk, and dependency information."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.3 Prioritize Requirements, printed pp. 86–90; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-13.html"},{"id":"q61","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"A regional service team plans to learn why customers abandon a claims process. Which preparation best supports elicitation?","opts":["Define what must be learned, identify relevant participants, and select techniques suited to the objective and context","Schedule a workshop with the largest available stakeholder group","Draft future-state screens and ask users to review them","Ask the sponsor to choose a preferred cause before sessions"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Preparation identifies the elicitation objective, stakeholders, and techniques suited to context.","A large workshop may exclude people; choose a format based on the objective and participants.","Screens assume a solution before the need and causes are understood.","Choosing a cause first can bias elicitation."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q62","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"A BA summarizes an interview. The stakeholder says one key exception was described inaccurately. What should the BA do?","opts":["Retain the note and flag the objection for later prioritization","Confirm the specific information with the stakeholder, correct the record, and recheck related conclusions","Ask another stakeholder to decide which account is more credible","Rewrite the exception as a solution requirement and continue"],"answer":[1],"why":["Prioritization does not correct inaccurate elicitation information.","Correct: Confirming with the source resolves whether the captured information reflects the intended meaning.","Another stakeholder cannot determine what the interviewee intended.","Recasting an unconfirmed interpretation as a requirement carries the inaccuracy forward."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q63","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"A customer says, “I need a simpler renewal.” What is the best next step when specifying and modeling this need?","opts":["Record “renewal must be simple” as a functional requirement","Select a standard renewal design and ask whether it is acceptable","Clarify the desired outcome and model relevant user actions, rules, or information","Ask developers to infer the simplest workflow"],"answer":[2],"why":["Repeating “simple” leaves the need subjective and unanalyzable.","A standard design is a solution proposal, not evidence that the need is understood.","Correct: Clarifying the need before modeling makes the requirement understandable and avoids mistaking a solution for the need.","Developers should not infer essential business behavior from ambiguity."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.1 Specify and Model Requirements, printed pp. 136–140; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q64","domains":[5],"level":1,"tag":"Need","q":"A requirement says the service must notify a customer when an application is incomplete. Which question best helps validate it?","opts":["Can the current messaging platform send the notification?","Has the notification wording been approved by communications?","Can it be delivered before the next release?","Does this requirement support the stakeholder need and intended business outcome?"],"answer":[3],"why":["Platform capability addresses feasibility, not whether this is the right requirement.","Approved wording does not show the requirement supports the intended outcome.","Schedule feasibility does not establish alignment with the need.","Correct: Validation assesses whether a requirement aligns with stakeholder needs and business objectives."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q65","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"Two requirements address the same need. One reduces frequent delays; the other is needed before a regulatory change. How should the BA support prioritization?","opts":["Assess relative value, urgency, risks, and dependencies with stakeholders before ranking","Rank the more frequent feature first because frequency is measurable","Prioritize the regulatory item automatically without examining dependencies","Rank only by estimated implementation effort"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Prioritization weighs multiple contextual factors; no single factor automatically determines ranking.","Frequency informs value but not urgency, risk, or dependencies.","A regulatory date may create urgency, but dependencies and impact still matter.","Effort informs planning but is insufficient as the sole basis."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.3 Prioritize Requirements, printed pp. 86–90; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q66","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"A team must elicit needs from frontline staff, customers, and a regulator. Which preparation is most appropriate?","opts":["Use one identical interview script and meeting format for everyone","Prepare objectives and suitable methods based on each group’s knowledge, access, and information needs","Begin with requirements from a similar project and ask stakeholders to approve them","Hold a regulator session first and treat its statements as the complete need"],"answer":[1],"why":["One format may not suit different roles, availability, and information needs.","Correct: Preparation is tailored to the objective, stakeholder characteristics, and context.","Reusing prior requirements can bias elicitation and may not fit this change.","Regulatory input may not represent all stakeholder needs or operational context."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q67","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"After a workshop, the recorded process differs from what one participant remembers. What is the best confirmation approach?","opts":["Use the facilitator’s recollection as authoritative","Keep both versions as approved alternatives","Review the disputed process detail and context with relevant participants, then update the record based on confirmation","Select the version easiest to model"],"answer":[2],"why":["Facilitator memory is not a substitute for confirming disputed information.","Correct: this review clarifies and corrects the elicited result.","Correct: Confirmation resolves discrepancies by checking captured results with relevant participants.","Recording both as approved preserves rather than resolves conflict."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q68","domains":[5],"level":2,"tag":"Need","q":"A policy has three exceptions and two user roles. Which specification is most useful for a requirement about deciding eligibility?","opts":["Write “eligibility must be handled correctly”","Use a screen mock-up showing the final result","Use a technical component diagram showing where code will run","Use a decision or rules model to make conditions, outcomes, and role differences explicit"],"answer":[3],"why":["“Correctly” does not identify conditions or outcomes.","A screen can show an outcome without explaining the rules.","A component diagram describes solution structure, not eligibility logic.","Correct: A decision or rules model makes conditions and outcomes explicit for analysis and shared understanding."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.1 Specify and Model Requirements, printed pp. 136–140; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q69","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"A requirement is feasible and has sponsor support, but may disadvantage a customer group. What should validation examine?","opts":["Whether it aligns with stakeholder needs and business objectives, including consequences for affected groups","Whether it is clear enough for developers to estimate","Whether it can be moved to the next release","Whether the sponsor has authority to accept the risk"],"answer":[0],"why":["Correct: Validation checks alignment with needs and objectives and whether consequences are acceptable in context.","Correct: this addresses alignment and impact on affected stakeholders.","Clarity and estimation do not establish appropriateness.","Release timing does not answer whether the requirement supports the need."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §7.3 Validate Requirements, printed pp. 144–147; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q70","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"A high-value requirement depends on a lower-value data migration. What should the analyst recommend?","opts":["Prioritize migration only if its standalone value exceeds the dependent requirement","Consider the dependency and sequencing when comparing both items’ priority and expected value","Drop the high-value requirement because it cannot be delivered independently","Give every requirement in the dependency chain equal priority"],"answer":[1],"why":["A prerequisite can enable value elsewhere, not only create its own value.","Correct: Dependencies affect priority and order even when a prerequisite has less standalone value.","Correct: dependencies influence relative priority and implementation sequence.","A dependency does not by itself invalidate the high-value item."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §5.3 Prioritize Requirements, printed pp. 86–90; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q71","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"A BA is preparing to elicit a need from executives who describe outcomes and staff who perform the process daily. What is the strongest plan?","opts":["Observe staff only because executives lack process detail","Ask executives to approve a detailed process model before meeting staff","Use an outcome-focused executive interview and process-focused staff observation or workshop, matched to objectives","Send the same broad survey to both and treat response volume as completeness"],"answer":[2],"why":["Executives may not validate operational detail before it is elicited.","Correct: complementary methods capture executive outcomes and operational detail.","Correct: Different stakeholder knowledge and objectives call for complementary techniques.","Staff observation alone may miss business outcomes and executive constraints."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.1 Prepare for Elicitation, printed pp. 56–60; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"},{"id":"q72","domains":[5],"level":3,"tag":"Need","q":"The BA confirmed interview facts, but stakeholders disagree about a term used in the results. What should happen before treating the results as shared?","opts":["Record the most common definition and proceed","Ask the sponsor to select a definition without discussion","Keep the term because participants approved the interview notes","Clarify the term and its context with relevant participants, then update the result"],"answer":[3],"why":["Majority usage may not match the intended meaning in context.","A sponsor decision may be needed, but first clarify ambiguity and perspectives.","Correct: clarifying meaning with participants resolves the discrepancy.","Correct: Confirmation establishes that the meaning of captured information is understood consistently."],"ref":"BABOK Guide v3 §4.3 Confirm Elicitation Results, printed pp. 65; ECBA Exam Blueprint v1.12 p. 2, Domain 5: Needs and Solutions","multi":false,"sourceSet":"ecba-20-timed-practice-set-14.html"}]);